he line through the midpoint of \\(BC\\) that is perpendicular to \\(BC\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF'\\) is the line through the midpoint of \\(BD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(BD\\).  Both mid\u2011points lie on the line \\(MN\\) (the line joining the centres of the original circles), because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) and the mid\u2011points of the chords \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are exactly the projections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) onto those chords.  Consequently the two perpendicular bisectors intersect at a point of \\(MN\\).  The third perpendicular bisector (that of \\(E'F'\\)) also passes through this intersection, for the three bisectors are concurrent.  Hence \\(O'\\in MN\\).\n\nInversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself (the line does not pass through the centre \\(A\\)), and it maps the centre \\(O'\\) of the image circle back to the centre \\(O\\) of the original circle \\((BEF)\\).  Therefore \\(O\\) also belongs to \\(MN\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  In any triangle the line through the orthocenter that is parallel to a given side is perpendicular to the opposite side; applying this to triangle \\(PMN\\) gives that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nLemma\u202f2 tells us that the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(MN\\); consequently the radius \\(OM\\) (or \\(ON\\)) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OM\\) of \\((BEF)\\).\n\nA line that is perpendicular to a radius at its endpoint is tangent to the circle at that endpoint (tangent\u2011radius theorem).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\u220e"
