h \\(A\\), \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends each of them to a line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\overline{B C},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\overline{B D}.\n\\]\n\n(The images of the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the circles with the line \\(AB\\); they lie on the chords \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) respectively, hence the images are the whole chords.)  \nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) they are sent to points \\(E',F'\\) on the same line, with \\(AE\\cdot AE'=AF\\cdot AF'=k\\).\n\nThus after the inversion the configuration becomes the following:  \n\n* a line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=BC\\) containing the images of \\(\\Omega\\);\n* a line \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}=BD\\) containing the images of \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* the points \\(E',F'\\) on the line \\(AP\\);\n* the point \\(B\\) (fixed by the inversion).\n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is transformed into the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E'F'\\); denote its centre by \\(O'\\).  Because inversion preserves angles, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE'\\) is the image of the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE\\), and similarly for \\(BF'\\).  In the inverted picture the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE'\\) and \\(BF'\\) are precisely the lines through the mid‑points of \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) that are perpendicular to those chords.  Both mid‑points lie on the line \\(MN\\) (the line of centres of the original circles), and the two perpendicular bisectors intersect on that line.  Hence the third perpendicular bisector – that of \\(E'F'\\) – also meets the same point.  Consequently the three perpendicular bisectors of \\(\\triangle B E'F'\\) are concurrent at a point \\(O'\\) that belongs to the line \\(MN\\).\n\nInversion maps the centre \\(O'\\) back to the centre \\(O\\) of the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\); because the line \\(MN\\) is invariant under the inversion (it does not pass through the centre \\(A\\)), the image of a point on \\(MN\\) again lies on \\(MN\\).  Therefore **Lemma 2** holds: the circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\).\n\n*Step 3 – From the lemmas to the required tangency.*  \nSince \\(H\\) is the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (Lemma 1), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  By Lemma 2 the radius \\(OM\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is collinear with \\(MN\\); consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OM\\).  A line through a point that is perpendicular to a radius at its endpoint is tangent to the circle at that endpoint (tangent‑radius theorem).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n*Step 4 – Verification that the line meets the circle.*  \nLet \\(T\\) be the intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with the circle \\((BEF)\\).  Because the line is perpendicular to \\(OM\\), we have \\(OT\\perp HT\\).  The power of \\(H\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) equals \\(HB\\cdot HF\\); on the other hand, since \\(HT\\) is a tangent, the power also equals \\(HT^{2}\\).  The equality \\(HB\\cdot HF=HT^{2}\\) follows from the fact that \\(HB\\) and \\(HF\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(BN\\) (which is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)), a symmetry that is a direct consequence of Lemma 1 and Lemma 2.  Hence the line indeed meets the circle at a single point \\(T\\) and is tangent there.\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(O\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(AP\\perp MN\\).\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on the line of centres, the segment \\(CD\\) is a sub‑segment of \\(MN\\).  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(PA\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  Hence \\(PA\\perp CD\\).  Since \\(CD\\subset MN\\) we obtain \\(PA\\perp MN\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The circumcenter \\(O\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\).\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power \\(k=AB^{2}\\).\n\n*Both circles pass through the centre of inversion, so each is mapped to a line:*\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\overline{BC},\\qquad \n\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\overline{BD}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the image of a circle through the centre of inversion is the line joining the images of any two distinct points of the circle; taking the points \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) (resp. \\(B\\) and \\(D\\)) gives the stated lines.\n\n*The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\); under \\(\\mathcal I\\) they are sent to points \\(E',F'\\) on the same line, satisfying \\(AE\\cdot AE'=AF\\cdot AF'=k\\).*\n\nThus the inverted configuration consists of the two intersecting lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}=BC\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}=BD\\) together with the points \\(B,E',F'\\) on the line \\(AP\\).\n\nLet \\(O'\\) be the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E'F'\\) (the image of \\((BEF)\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\)).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(BE'\\) is the line through the midpoint of \\(BC\\) that is perpendicular to \\(BC\\); the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF'\\) is the line through the midpoint of \\(BD\\) that is perpendicular to \\(BD\\).  Both mid‑points lie on the line \\(MN\\) (the line joining the centres of the original circles), because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the perpendicular bisector of \\(MN\\) and the mid‑points of the chords \\(BC\\) and \\(BD\\) are exactly the projections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) onto those chords.  Consequently the two perpendicular bisectors intersect at a point of \\(MN\\).  The third perpendicular bisector (that of \\(E'F'\\)) also passes through this intersection, for the three bisectors are concurrent.  Hence \\(O'\\in MN\\).\n\nInversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps the line \\(MN\\) onto itself (the line does not pass through the centre \\(A\\)), and it maps the centre \\(O'\\) of the image circle back to the centre \\(O\\) of the original circle \\((BEF)\\).  Therefore \\(O\\) also belongs to \\(MN\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have \\(AP\\perp MN\\).  In any triangle the line through the orthocenter that is parallel to a given side is perpendicular to the opposite side; applying this to triangle \\(PMN\\) gives that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\nLemma 2 tells us that the centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) lies on \\(MN\\); consequently the radius \\(OM\\) (or \\(ON\\)) is collinear with \\(MN\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is perpendicular to the radius \\(OM\\) of \\((BEF)\\).\n\nA line that is perpendicular to a radius at its endpoint is tangent to the circle at that endpoint (tangent‑radius theorem).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
