e of inversion, its image is the *polar* of the centre with respect to that circle.  \nThe polar of a point \\(X\\) with respect to a circle of centre \\(O\\) is the line through the other intersection of any chord through \\(X\\) with the circle and perpendicular to \\(OX\\).\n\n* The polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) is the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(AM\\); denote it \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).  \n  Hence \\(\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).\n\n* The polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\Gamma\\) is the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(AN\\); denote it \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n  Hence \\(\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\); a line through the centre of inversion is invariant under the inversion, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(E)=E',\\qquad \\mathcal I(F)=F',\\qquad\\text{with }E',F'\\in AP .\n\\]\n\nThus the inverted configuration consists of the two intersecting lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) meeting at \\(B\\) and a third line \\(AP\\) meeting them at the points \\(E',F'\\).\n\nLet \\(O'\\) be the centre of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BE'F'\\) (the image of \\((BEF)\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\)).  \nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(BE'\\) is the line through the midpoint of the segment joining the foot of the perpendicular from \\(M\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\); this line is parallel to \\(MN\\).  \nSimilarly, the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF'\\) is the line through the foot of the perpendicular from \\(N\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); it is also parallel to \\(MN\\).\n\nConsequently the two bisectors intersect on a line parallel to \\(MN\\).  Because both bisectors are perpendicular respectively to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), their intersection is the unique point that is simultaneously at equal distances from the two lines \\(\