center \\(H\\) that is parallel to a side (\\(AP\\)) is perpendicular to the opposite side, therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Lemma 2 tells us that the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); thus the segment \\(KO\\) is a radius of \\((BEF)\\) and is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with \\(KO\\); by the tangent‑radius theorem it is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|MA|<|NA|\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C-M-N-D\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\));\n* \\(H\\) the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\);\n* \\(K\\) the midpoint of the segment \\(MN\\);\n* \\(O\\) the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1.  \\(AP\\perp MN\\).\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line of centres, the chord \\(CD\\) is a sub‑segment of \\(MN\\).  \n\\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\).  \nConsequently the line \\(PA\\) passes through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and is orthogonal to \\(CD\\).  Since \\(CD\\subset MN\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN .\n\\]\n\nThe two circles intersect in two distinct points, hence the line of centres cannot pass through either intersection point; therefore \\(A\\notin MN\\) and the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is well defined.\n\n∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2.  The centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\) lies on the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AB^{2}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Images of the given objects\n\n*Because a circle passing through the centre of inversion is transformed into its polar line*, the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are sent to the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma),\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)) is the line through \\(B\\) perpendicular to \\(AM\\) (resp. \\(AN\\)).  \nIndeed, for a circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\) that contains \\(A\\), the polar of \\(M\\) with respect to the inversion circle is the line through the two inverse images of the points where the circle meets the ray \\(AM\\); those two points are symmetric with respect to the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(AM\\), whence the polar is perpendicular to \\(AM\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant; denote by \\(E',F'\\) the images of \\(E,F\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\nE',F'\\in AP ,\\qquad  \\mathcal I(\\,BEF\\,)=\\; \\text{circumcircle of }\\triangle BE'F'.\n\\]\n\nLet \\(O'\\) be the centre of that circumcircle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The points \\(M_{0},N_{0}\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM_{0}= \\text{foot of the perpendicular from }M\\text{ to }\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nN_{0}= \\text{foot of the perpendicular from }N\\text{ to }\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\perp AM\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\perp AN\\), the right‑angled quadrilaterals \\(AMM_{0}B\\) and \\(ANN_{0}B\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  Consequently \\(M_{0}\\) and \\(N_{0}\\) are the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the line \\(AP\\); in particular they are symmetric with respect to the midpoint \\(K\\) of \\(MN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE'\\) and \\(BF'\\)\n\nIn the right‑angled triangle \\(BM_{0}E'\\) we have \\(\\angle BM_{0}E'=90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the midpoint of \\(BE'\\) lies on the line through \\(M_{0}\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence the perpendicular bisector of \\(BE'\\) is exactly the line  \n\n\\[\n\\beta_{1}= \\{\\,X\\mid X\\text{ lies on the line through }M_{0}\\text{ parallel to }AP\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAn analogous argument shows that the perpendicular bisector of \\(BF'\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\beta_{2}= \\{\\,X\\mid X\\text{ lies on the line through }N_{0}\\text{ parallel to }AP\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M_{0}\\) and \\(N_{0}\\) are symmetric about \\(K\\), the two lines \\(\\beta_{1}\\) and \\(\\beta_{2}\\) intersect at a point \\(O'\\) that belongs to the unique line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Thus  \n\n\\[\nO'\\in \\ell_{K},\\qquad \\ell_{K}:=\\text{the line through }K\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Invariance of \\(\\ell_{K}\\)\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on both the line \\(MN\\) and the circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\); therefore they are fixed by the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\).  Both \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) belong to \\(\\ell_{K}\\) (by definition of \\(K\\) as the midpoint of \\(MN\\)).  For any point \\(X\\in\\ell_{K}\\setminus\\{C,D\\}\\) the ray \\(AX\\) meets the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) at two points whose inverses are again on the same ray; consequently the product \\(AX\\cdot AX'\\) equals the constant power \\(k\\).  Hence the image of \\(X\\) under \\(\\mathcal I\\) also lies on \\(\\ell_{K}\\).  Therefore the whole line \\(\\ell_{K}\\) is invariant as a set under the inversion.\n\nSince