\cdot AP'=k=AB^{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\); after inversion they become the points \\(C'=\\mathcal I(C)\\) and \\(D'=\\mathcal I(D)\\) which lie on the same line \\(\\ell\\).  \nMoreover, because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\) (they are the second intersections of \\(MN\\) with the two circles that both contain \\(A\\)), their images \\(C',D'\\) are also symmetric with respect to \\(AP\\).  \n\nConsequently the line \\(\\ell\\) is the *polar* of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((ACD)\\); the polar passes through the two inverse points \\(C',D'\\) and is orthogonal to the line joining \\(A\\) with the centre \\(P\\).  Hence \\(AP\\) is the *symmedian* of triangle \\(ABM\\) (and similarly of \\(ABN\\)), i.e. the isogonal image of the median \\(AM\\) (resp. \\(AN\\)).  In particular the cross‑ratio  \n\n\\[\n(A,B;E,F)=-1,\n\\]\n\nso the quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is harmonic.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.  The centre \\(O\\) of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) lies on the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nApply the same inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\).  \nThe circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) become the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) described above.  \nThe points \\(E,F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\); therefore their images \\(E'=\\mathcal I(E)\\) and \\(F'=\\mathcal I(F)\\) also lie on \\(AP\\).  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nM_{0}= \\text{foot of the perpendicular from }M\\text{ to }\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \nN_{0}= \\text{foot of the perpendicular from }N\\text{ to }\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\perp AM\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\perp AN\\), the quadrilaterals \\(AMM_{0}B\\) and \\(ANN_{0}B\\) are right‑angled at \\(M_{0}\\) and \\(N_{0}\\); consequently \\(M_{0}\\) and \\(N_{0}\\) are the reflections of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) in the line \\(AP\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nM_{0} \\text{